and go to the second iteration and the third iteration. And that's how the first machine

00:21:05.719 --> 00:21:09.719
learning was implemented. And the second machine learning was implemented. So it was like a very

00:21:09.719 --> 00:21:17.319
hand in hand and very close work to be able to achieve what we achieved. But I think one of the

00:21:17.319 --> 00:21:22.599
good things is we didn't want it to have the perfect answer in the very beginning because we

00:21:22.599 --> 00:21:27.319
didn't know what the perfect answer is even, because again, I taught triaging with the, you

00:21:27.319 --> 00:21:34.680
know, probability of the outcomes. That would be the answer, but probably that wasn't the bottleneck

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:39.880
necessarily. Maybe the bottleneck was like having some visibility of how staffs are dealing with

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:45.880
each case perhaps. So again, some of the things we don't know, probably stakeholders might not know

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:50.520
either, sadly. So we have to like work together and learn this together. Yeah.

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:55.160
It's a conversation, communication. All right. Well, we have so many questions in Slido.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:59.959
If we do not start asking them, we will never get through any of them. Let's do it.

00:21:59.959 --> 00:22:02.839
Yeah. Romina, I get so excited talking about this.

00:22:02.839 --> 00:22:07.319
No, it's been wonderful. And we have a question. In fact, the one that has bubbled to the top,

00:22:07.319 --> 00:22:12.680
because reminder, everybody, you can upload questions, is right in line with what Romina

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:16.359
was talking about a little bit, because the question says, in your experience,

00:22:16.359 --> 00:22:21.880
what are the key indicators that a problem is a good candidate for machine learning rather than

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:27.400
a simpler statistical approach? And that sort of iterative process is probably part of that.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.079
Who wants to take this one? Is this a Romina question?

00:22:31.079 --> 00:22:36.680
I can take that one. I think it's about what's the question we are answering. So here we did

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:44.359
wanted to have a more predictive workflow instead of just like, because statistically,

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:51.160
if we want to only use a statistical point of view, you're only looking at historic data.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:58.760
You're not looking at the future information. And I think what at least we wanted to influence,

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:06.359
it was like maybe some predictive analysis would be an answer. And I think that's the way we kind

00:23:06.359 --> 00:23:12.680
of took it on this case. Again, we touch base many, many, many, many things. And for instance,

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:18.680
Amanda does a lot of ROIs and causal analysis and more of a stat side. But because we're now

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:24.199
focusing on the impact award and the project we did, which is, again, one of the biggest ones

00:23:24.199 --> 00:23:29.560
throughout the company as well, this is the approach we took. I hope I answered the question.

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:35.160
I'll be happy to answer any follow-ups. There are plenty more questions for that

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:41.719
case. Do not worry. Hubert had a question. And if he could ask that live, that would be amazing.

00:23:41.719 --> 00:23:49.400
And I will stick it in the chat as well. We'll get Hubert unmuted in two seconds here.

00:23:54.199 --> 00:23:58.199
Oh, my computer is telling me I need to update. Hold on. I'm ignoring that.

00:23:58.199 --> 00:24:02.280
Sorry. That was my being slow there. There we go.

00:24:03.239 --> 00:24:10.439
There I am. Yeah. So this is really interesting. And I think my question is,

00:24:10.439 --> 00:24:17.160
what kind of data sources do you use? You talk about claims data, EHR data, other kind of data,

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:22.599
you've got provider-based data, or some of those, I mean, you've already talked some of those PDFs

00:24:22.599 --> 00:24:29.000
and TIFFs and all the crazy data challenges, but what are those data challenges, some of those

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:34.839
tough data challenges that you've had and how have you solved them? And is it data challenges

00:24:34.839 --> 00:24:44.839
in terms of availability or structure of data or both? Everything. That's a great question,

00:24:44.839 --> 00:24:50.439
because that's what I struggle with since the first day I started here. So when I first started,

00:24:50.439 --> 00:24:56.040
initially, I thought that the main data issue was going to be the fact that we primarily rely

00:24:56.040 --> 00:25:02.359
on claims data, which has about, it can have up to a three-month lag before we can say,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:08.680
okay, we've, it's been enough time. If a claim happened, it would have been in our system. Now

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:14.359
they have that amount of time. So we always have to take into account a claims lag when we do our

00:25:14.359 --> 00:25:20.680
analyses. But then as I started getting into more ROIs, program evals, building things, it was

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:28.280
actually the sources of data, the quality of the data, not knowing the lineage of the data,

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:34.040
having to ask like 20 people, hey, did you make this table? How did you make this table? Where's

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:40.119
the code? So I kind of had to do my detective work to get to the top to find what are our sources of

00:25:40.119 --> 00:25:47.000
truth. And I feel like that's a big struggle because there are, we do have a lot of different

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:58.599
vendors. So for example, for, for the, this project and Metatrack, which is a vendor,

00:25:58.599 --> 00:26:04.359
but in a way that's good because then we get source of truth, reliable data from them every

00:26:04.359 --> 00:26:10.280
night. So we can pull directly from those tables. On other projects that we're working on, they use

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:17.319
a vendor Jiva for their UI and management. So then we're able to pull directly that Jiva data.

00:26:18.439 --> 00:26:28.839
The, the, the tricky part is the data storage, the data, you know, there's no oversight on folks

00:26:28.839 --> 00:26:35.319
making tables. So it can be a little bit tricky, but my solution where now I feel good about it,

00:26:35.319 --> 00:26:40.680
and I don't have a heart attack is that I'm making sure that when I'm pulling in data,

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:45.959
I'm like, I'm from, I know where that data came from. I know that it's a direct ingestion from

00:26:45.959 --> 00:26:52.920
a vendor. I know that it's coming directly from a state file. And finding the locations also can

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:59.560
be difficult. Romina can also speak to that. I mean, with the PDF files or the TIFF files,

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:06.040
they are stored in some insane locations. So it really is, I think, from an organizational

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:18.359
structure, the data governance, maybe we want to call it, it's just not where I would love it to

00:27:18.359 --> 00:27:24.199
be. I mean, I come from academia where, you know, data and gathering data and storing data was a

00:27:24.199 --> 00:27:32.199
lot simpler. So it was a huge, it was a huge challenge. So for me, I just have to always ask

00:27:32.199 --> 00:27:38.599
myself, what is this, where did this table come from? Is this the original ingestion? Because I

00:27:38.599 --> 00:27:43.719
don't want a table that somebody else has, has messed with, which has, has been the biggest

00:27:43.719 --> 00:27:50.280
headache. Amazing question, Hubert, thank you for taking us in a good direction. Lots of conversation

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:56.839
in the chat about, in data quality or whether or not third-party data is reliable at all.

00:27:56.839 --> 00:28:04.280
And I want to make sure as well that we define TIFF out loud. I define it in the chat, but a

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:10.839
.TIFF file is just an image file, which is a difficult thing to parse. Yeah. When it's full

00:28:10.839 --> 00:28:17.239
of information, PDFs are hard enough, but image files are notoriously, notoriously difficult when

00:28:17.239 --> 00:28:25.000
you have them as your sources of input. Okay. So we have some more questions here. There is one

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:31.640
that I would love to ask both of you just to get a little bit of context about the way that we work

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:39.560
here. Wait, I lost it because things moved around in Slido. Aha, it was Zach's question. I have put

00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:42.760
it to the top here and I will put it in the chat as well. It's got a little asterisk, so I'm going

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:49.400
to read it for Zach. And Zach says, after automating a lot of processes, do you find

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:55.239
yourself with not much work to do? And if so, what would you do in that kind of situation?

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:59.319
I don't know if this is ever a problem for you. There's probably more and more stuff to fix,

00:28:59.319 --> 00:29:04.599
right? Like you said, there was like a portal that you hadn't even gotten your hands on yet, right?

